Below is a **self‑contained JavaScript module** that implements Bubble Sort for an array of integers.  

* The function `bubbleSort(arr)` validates the input (type, length, value range).  
* If the input is invalid it throws a descriptive `Error`.  
* The algorithm itself is the classic Bubble Sort – it repeatedly swaps adjacent out‑of‑order elements until the whole list is sorted.  
* Time‑complexity: **O(n²)** in the worst and average case, **O(n)** in the best case (already sorted) because of the early‑exit flag.  
* Space‑complexity: **O(1)** – sorting is done in‑place.

```js
/**
 * Bubble Sort implementation with full input validation.
 *
 * @param {Array<number>} arr - The array of integers to sort.
 * @returns {Array<number>} The same array instance, now sorted in ascending order.
 *
 * @throws {TypeError}   If `arr` is not an array.
 * @throws {RangeError}  If the array length exceeds 100 elements.
 * @throws {RangeError}  If any element is not an integer or is outside [-10000, 10000].
 *
 * @example
 *   const data = [5, -2, 9, 0, 3];
 *   console.log(bubbleSort(data)); // [-2, 0, 3, 5, 9]
 */
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  // ---------- Input validation ----------
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError('Input must be an array.');
  }

  const MAX_LENGTH = 100;
  const MIN_VALUE = -10000;
  const MAX_VALUE = 10000;

  if (arr.length > MAX_LENGTH) {
    throw new RangeError(`Array length must not exceed ${MAX_LENGTH} elements (got ${arr.length}).`);
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const val = arr[i];
    if (!Number.isInteger(val)) {
      throw new RangeError(`Element at index ${i} is not an integer: ${val}`);
    }
    if (val < MIN_VALUE || val > MAX_VALUE) {
      throw new RangeError(
        `Element at index ${i} (${val}) is out of allowed range [${MIN_VALUE}, ${MAX_VALUE}].`
      );
    }
  }

  // ---------- Bubble Sort ----------
  const n = arr.length;
  // If the array is empty or has a single element it is already sorted.
  if (n < 2) return arr;

  // Outer loop runs until no swaps are made (array is sorted)
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    // `swapped` tracks whether any exchange happened in this pass.
    let swapped = false;

    // After each outer iteration, the largest element among the unsorted part
    // bubbles to its final position at index `n - 1 - i`. Hence we can stop
    // the inner loop one element earlier each time.
    for (let j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // Swap adjacent elements
        const temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
        swapped = true;
      }
    }

    // If no two elements were swapped, the array is already sorted.
    if (!swapped) break;
  }

  return arr; // sorted in‑place
}

// ------------------- Example usage -------------------
try {
  const input = [34, -5, 23, 0, 12, -10000, 9999];
  console.log('Original:', input);
  const sorted = bubbleSort(input);
  console.log('Sorted  :', sorted);
} catch (e) {
  console.error(e.name + ': ' + e.message);
}
```

### How the algorithm works
1. **Validation** – Before sorting we ensure the argument is an array, its length ≤ 100, and every element is an integer within the allowed range.  
2. **Outer loop** – Repeats passes over the array. After each pass the largest unsorted element is placed at its final position, so the inner loop’s range shrinks (`n‑1‑i`).  
3. **Inner loop** – Compares each pair of adjacent items; if they are out of order, they are swapped.  
4. **Early exit** – If a full pass completes without any swap (`swapped` stays `false`), the array is already sorted and the algorithm stops early, giving the best‑case **O(n)** performance.  

